urge|strongly-encourage|try hard to persuade or advise someone to act|urge someone to do something; urge that|A|Both sources describe forceful encouragement to act; keep this transitive verb meaning distinct from the feeling of an urge, which is not supported in this group.
divide|split-into-parts|separate one whole into smaller parts|divide something into equal parts|A|Oxford gives dividing a cake and Vocab4600 supports splitting something; this is a concrete transitive verb use, separate from social conflict.
divide|cause-disagreement|create opposing views or separation within a group|an issue divides a community|B|Oxford directly shows an issue dividing a community; preserve the figurative verb sense because it describes disagreement rather than physical partition.
divide|social-difference|a social or economic difference separating groups|a divide between rich and poor|C|Oxford uses the noun divide for a difference between rich and poor; this abstract noun is distinct from the two verbal constructions.
economy|regional-economic-system|the financial and business activity of a country or region|local economy; national economy|A|Both linked sources describe the money and businesses of a region; examples about tourism and jobs support one noun sense without adding economic theories.
embrace|hug-physically|hold someone closely with the arms|embrace someone; embrace at a place|A|Oxford illustrates friends embracing and Vocab4600 glosses the word as hug; this literal verb sense is separate from accepting an idea.
embrace|accept-idea|willingly adopt or welcome an idea, method, or change|embrace new technology; embrace a change|B|Oxford shows a company embracing new technology; retain this figurative verb because it is source-backed and clearly differs from a physical hug.
dependence|reliance-on-support|a condition of relying on someone or something|dependence on fuel; dependence on a person|A|Both sources describe reliance, with Oxford examples involving fuel and an instructor; keep one broad noun sense across people and resources.
suite|hotel-room-set|a group of connected rooms used together, especially in a hotel|book a suite; a suite with bedrooms|A|Oxford gives a hotel suite and Vocab4600 defines a set of rooms; this countable accommodation sense has direct bilingual support.
suite|software-package|a collection of related software applications or tools|a design suite; software suite includes tools|B|Oxford explicitly uses design suite for editing tools; retain the established software-package meaning as a separate countable noun sense.
remarkably|surprisingly-to-degree|to an unusual or surprising degree that attracts attention|remarkably quickly; remarkably stable|A|Oxford examples modify quickly and stable, while the second source defines surprising notice; one degree adverb use covers both.
prove|show-truth|show through evidence or a test that a statement is true|prove that a claim is true; figures prove|A|The linked sources and examples consistently support demonstrating truth with evidence; keep this transitive or clause-taking verb use.
prove|turn-out-to-be|be discovered through experience to have a particular quality|prove difficult; prove safe|B|Oxford contrasts a repair proving difficult with a test proving safety; this resultative construction differs from proving a proposition.
shoot|fire-weapon|fire a weapon so that a projectile leaves it|shoot at a target; shoot a weapon|A|Both linked sources directly support firing at a target; keep the verb construction clear and use a non-instructional example.
shoot|record-image|use a camera to make photographs or film footage|shoot a portrait; shoot a scene|B|Oxford supports photographing portraits and filming; this verb sense is clearly separate from firing a weapon and the noun for a session.
shoot|photo-film-session|an organized session for making photographs or film footage|a fashion shoot; a film shoot|C|Oxford has separate fashion and film shoot examples; define the noun as a scheduled production session, not the act expressed by the verb.
subscriber|paid-content-user|a person paying for recurring publication or online access|magazine subscriber; digital subscriber|A|Oxford and Vocab4600 describe a person receiving publications or online access, usually for payment; this is one noun sense across formats.
harvest|collected-crops|the crops or produce gathered from a farm in a season|rice harvest; apple harvest|A|Oxford uses rice harvest as the crop result; define the noun as produce collected rather than the work of collecting it.
harvest|gathering-season|the activity or period of collecting crops from fields|during harvest; harvest begins|B|Vocab4600 identifies the act of collecting farmed food; separate the work or season from the collected produce when the source supports both.
harvest|gather-crops|collect mature crops from fields or trees|harvest grapes; harvest crops|C|Oxford directly supports farmers harvesting grapes; this transitive verb is distinct from the two related noun readings.
gap|physical-opening|an open space or break between objects or surfaces|a gap under a door; gap in a fence|A|Oxford examples show spaces under a door and in a fence; Vocab4600 also supports a space between things, so keep one physical noun sense.
gap|difference-between-groups|a measurable difference or separation between people or groups|age gap; gap between rich and poor|B|Oxford explicitly supports age and wealth differences; this abstract reading is separate from a physical opening.
gap|free-interval|a period with no scheduled activity between events|a gap in a schedule; gap after lunch|C|Oxford gives an unscheduled interval in a person's schedule; retain it as a time-related noun sense distinct from spaces and disparities.
relaxing|causing-rest|making someone feel calm or less tense|a relaxing bath; relaxing evening|A|Oxford examples describe an activity or setting that helps a person rest; the adjective is supported by both source examples without extra claims.
legend|traditional-story|a traditional story about past events or people|local legend; according to legend|A|Oxford examples describe a local tale and a story told as tradition; Vocab4600 directly supports a story from the past.
legend|admired-person|a highly famous and admired person in a field|a legend in tennis; sporting legend|B|Oxford directly uses legend for a celebrated tennis figure; keep this figurative noun distinct from the traditional-story meaning.
consecutive|unbroken-series|following one after another without an interruption|consecutive games; consecutive days|A|Both linked sources describe an uninterrupted sequence; examples of games, days, and hours support one adjective meaning.
splash|scatter-liquid-on-impact|cause liquid to hit a surface and spread or scatter|paint splashes on a floor; splash water|A|Vocab4600 defines liquid striking something and spreading, with paint on a floor as evidence; author only this source-supported verb meaning.
lush|abundant-healthy-growth|covered with many large, healthy plants|lush jungle; lush vegetation|A|The linked source describes healthy plant growth and gives a jungle example; keep the adjective focused on abundant vegetation.
everyone|all-people-in-group|every person in a particular group|everyone enjoyed; everyone needs|A|Oxford supports the pronoun referring to all people; examples show subject use and the singular agreement pattern.
torment|cause-deliberate-suffering|cause someone prolonged distress or suffering, deliberately or intensely|torment someone; be tormented by guilt|A|Vocab4600 says to cause someone to suffer on purpose and gives insecurity as the cause; keep the verb sense focused on serious distress.
beg|ask-earnestly|ask someone very strongly for permission, help, or action|beg someone to do something; beg for help|A|Oxford examples show an urgent request to parents or a driver; keep the transitive request pattern and avoid adding unsupported meanings.
